// @kama/redis — the Redis and Valkey client for kama. It speaks RESP2 and RESP3 directly, in kama, over
// std::net; there is no hiredis and nothing to install. TLS will come from @kama/tls.
//
// The shape of the package, as it fills in (docs/ROADMAP.md):
//   redis            Connection (src/connection.kama), Config (src/config.kama), RedisError, ServerError and
//                    ServerCode (src/error.kama), Command (src/command.kama), RedisArg and the `cmd"…"` tag
//                    (src/arg.kama), `reply::<T>` and its kin (src/reply.kama), Pipeline and Replies
//                    (src/pipeline.kama), TxOutcome and TxBody (src/transaction.kama), Script and ScriptCall
//                    (src/script.kama), Scan (src/scan.kama), Subscriber, Delivery, Message and ChannelKind
//                    (src/subscriber.kama), ServerInfo (src/server.kama), the Transport and Dialer contracts
//                    (src/transport.kama), RedisUrl (src/url.kama)
//   redis::wire      internal: a deadline, RESP over a transport and what a value holds in memory, the commands a
//                    connection does not carry and the blocking commands' timeouts (src/wire/known.kama), the
//                    server's integer grammar
//   redis::resp      the protocol: RESP2 and RESP3 values, their reader and writer
//   redis::cluster   key slots (src/cluster/slot.kama); to come: the slot map and redirections
//   redis::pool      a connection pool shared by isolates
//
// The rules every module holds:
//   - Nothing from the server is trusted: every length is bounded before it is used, and a malformed reply
//     is `RedisError::Protocol`, never a panic.
//   - A data error is a `Result`; a contract violation (an index out of range) panics, exactly like an index
//     out of range on a collection.
//   - Non-blocking I/O with deadlines. A connection belongs to one isolate at a time and moves between them.
